ber_new_001_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A student argues: $1+\\frac{1}{n}\\to1$ and $1^n=1$, so $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(1+\\frac{1}{n}\\right)^n=1$. What is wrong with this argument?","options":["Limit laws cover a fixed exponent, but here the exponent grows.","Nothing: the limit is $1$, and $\\mathrm{e}$ is defined by another sequence.","$1+\\frac{1}{n}$ does not tend to $1$, because it always stays above $1$.","$1^n$ tends to $+\\infty$ as $n\\to\\infty$, so the limit is $+\\infty$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ccc4318b4b9b80664456964f","time_limit":40},{"id":"expansion_pack_020_eulers_number_new_007_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement about Euler's number $\\mathrm{e}$ is correct?","options":["It is the base of $x\\mapsto\\mathrm{e}^x$ and of $\\ln$.","It is the base of the common logarithm $\\log_{10}$.","The exponent laws hold only for the base $\\mathrm{e}$.","Every limit of the form $1^{\\infty}$ has the value $\\mathrm{e}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c65b626ea5ec7e0b7da3a072","time_limit":30},{"id":"expansion_pack_020_eulers_number_new_008_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(1-\\frac{2}{n}\\right)^n$?","options":["$\\mathrm{e}^{-2}$","$-\\mathrm{e}^{2}$","$\\mathrm{e}^{-1/2}$","$-2\\mathrm{e}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"790cc4f6a174aae328efac0b","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_eulers_number_e4344c76_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $a_n=\\left(1+\\frac{1}{n}\\right)^n$ for $n\\geq1$, so that $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}a_n=\\mathrm{e}$. Which statement about the terms $a_n$ is correct?","options":["They decrease towards $\\mathrm{e}$, so every term is larger than $\\mathrm{e}$.","They increase towards $\\mathrm{e}$, and every term is less than $\\mathrm{e}$.","They reach the value $\\mathrm{e}$ exactly once $n$ is large enough.","They grow without bound, since the base exceeds $1$ and the exponent grows."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1a1ef44646d04db0961cd809","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_eulers_number_e4344c76_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Given that $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(1+\\frac{1}{n}\\right)^n=\\mathrm{e}$, what is $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(1+\\frac{1}{n}\\right)^{2n}$?","options":["$\\mathrm{e}$","$2\\mathrm{e}$","$+\\infty$","$\\mathrm{e}^{2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a6e088d3e4d286c878a9bd66","time_limit":30}]}}